 {:db/id "q-131116/ma-source-field/5db62383c700c871a14ce96fbb1c91484e04ef928dce2ebef99fd9d5f9a4bcd4" :answer-field/id #uuid "530f90fa-b927-569c-b4dd-79831f2442af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-131116/ma-source-field/5db62383c700c871a14ce96fbb1c91484e04ef928dce2ebef99fd9d5f9a4bcd4/d5eaaf07f0505e9d5b231b8075c51e274f896039e198fc1181cc54f3b52b276e" "q-131116/ma-source-field/5db62383c700c871a14ce96fbb1c91484e04ef928dce2ebef99fd9d5f9a4bcd4/947da72898dd46ce941403ebe7333a79145120cefb4cbf008e02fe37a359af49" "q-131116/ma-source-field/5db62383c700c871a14ce96fbb1c91484e04ef928dce2ebef99fd9d5f9a4bcd4/07f4227f1fca5f7d6a41fe60d797c0c31d6108906270b35c6b6f12f706f549c9" "q-131116/ma-source-field/5db62383c700c871a14ce96fbb1c91484e04ef928dce2ebef99fd9d5f9a4bcd4/7fc44339767192def569051958b4da8a6320146f6867510d397160a8bafc7f59" "q-131116/ma-source-field/5db62383c700c871a14ce96fbb1c91484e04ef928dce2ebef99fd9d5f9a4bcd4/620152572f25d2e6461570ce4b61cd78d53815be5338059714b5504833a0afbb"] :answer-field/correct "q-131116/ma-source-field/5db62383c700c871a14ce96fbb1c91484e04ef928dce2ebef99fd9d5f9a4bcd4/7fc44339767192def569051958b4da8a6320146f6867510d397160a8bafc7f59" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-131116" :question/id #uuid "da3be12b-b270-5258-9b1c-409842422d29" :question/math-academy-id "q-131116" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\text{ }i+2{z}^{2}y\\text{ }j+(2z{y}^{2}+2)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,-1) & =3(1)^{2}\\,i+2(-1)^{2}(-1)\\,j+(2(-1)(-1)^{2}+2)k \\\\  & =3\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3\\,i-2\\,j$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-131116/ma-source-field/5db62383c700c871a14ce96fbb1c91484e04ef928dce2ebef99fd9d5f9a4bcd4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
