 {:db/id "q-139454" :question/id #uuid "3c94d6f3-17f8-58e7-b287-f9a855b746d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-139454" :question/problem "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+4I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,(A+4I)^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-4,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A+4I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A+4I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A+4I)^{3}v=0.$ We're given that\n\n$$\n(A+4I)^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{3}$ is a solution to $(A+4I)^{3}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A+4I)^{3}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A+4I)^{2}v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\n(A+4I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that the solution to $(A+4I)^{2}v=0$ is ${v}_{1}=0,$ where ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A+4I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A+4I)^{3}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A+4I)^{2}v=0.$ \n\nHence, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ of $A.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-139454/ma-source-field/9883ce04a6b031a9b5ca76f96c2375889765c56c629bdcd3b14d844f2c2987a1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
