 {:db/id "q-140117/ma-source-field/c05c4fdefff749bf1ca44db3b9f1a058be9387376582fe202ed1101610958b99" :answer-field/id #uuid "f74f3682-41ce-5a31-9b35-9471ffa05fed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-140117/ma-source-field/c05c4fdefff749bf1ca44db3b9f1a058be9387376582fe202ed1101610958b99/b5af170cb1a071e9e1186efb567bab86ed0f83ee17fe770cfa72a6acd38547b4" "q-140117/ma-source-field/c05c4fdefff749bf1ca44db3b9f1a058be9387376582fe202ed1101610958b99/69110279b97442a4f79d77aa23e4ceb6d2729b686fed66030ab82dbc259a7b71" "q-140117/ma-source-field/c05c4fdefff749bf1ca44db3b9f1a058be9387376582fe202ed1101610958b99/1091a26a6bb7c58e5ddcc949bf49b622e5d748db3973c68cb89d6460831de0a7" "q-140117/ma-source-field/c05c4fdefff749bf1ca44db3b9f1a058be9387376582fe202ed1101610958b99/de10d9c21f2abd99bbc7ec87649eb80885fbb611fd99a530f20ab6b963895aeb" "q-140117/ma-source-field/c05c4fdefff749bf1ca44db3b9f1a058be9387376582fe202ed1101610958b99/5e56c231163065200378f879837f84bd4f17a016abb79c1aea27e5def4688984"] :answer-field/correct "q-140117/ma-source-field/c05c4fdefff749bf1ca44db3b9f1a058be9387376582fe202ed1101610958b99/1091a26a6bb7c58e5ddcc949bf49b622e5d748db3973c68cb89d6460831de0a7" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-140117" :question/id #uuid "d62d6869-fd0d-50a4-9c77-6fb492970304" :question/math-academy-id "q-140117" :question/problem "Let $x=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ If $x=y+z,$ where $y\\in \\text{Span}{a}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}{a},$ then $z=$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d8/d887852fe51f08cb7296437e69b6a9632721cfd155558e6abf9a99b4f59f7ad6.png)\n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{a}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{a\\cdot x}{a\\cdot a}a \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-5)+6\\cdot 5+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+4\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{-10+30+6+4}{4+36+4+16}a \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{60}a \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}a \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ z & =x-{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $y\\in \\text{Span}{a}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}{a},$ as shown in the picture above.\n\nIn conclusion, $z=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140117/ma-source-field/c05c4fdefff749bf1ca44db3b9f1a058be9387376582fe202ed1101610958b99"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
