 {:db/id "q-140579" :question/id #uuid "32164094-a2a0-521c-8478-fb3407c48f4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140579" :question/problem "![](images/a7/a71e2e5e4cd2fddac779a7c2d21d072663f406bc6595ef9c7e15c885bbb83889.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=1-\\frac{4}{x+3}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=5$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{-2}^{a}(1-\\frac{4}{x+3})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{a}^{5}(1-\\frac{4}{x+3})\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=1-\\frac{4}{x+3}.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{4}{x+3} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{x+3} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{x+3} & =1 \\\\ 4 & =x+3 \\\\ 1 & =x \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1\\in [-2,5],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=5.$\n\nSo, $a=1$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{-2}^{1}(1-\\frac{4}{x+3})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{5}(1-\\frac{4}{x+3})\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140579/ma-source-field/1b62fec68668a05d86c88b30df6669b995b10e8ca3b48c329c08f9c91491cd46"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
