 {:db/id "q-162581/ma-source-field/318c12ea7c36f63e2939bbed536f9dc1848f8aa2cd199a0b7b35c2dbd765c15b" :answer-field/id #uuid "196954bd-17a6-525e-a3ea-36557f290435" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-162581/ma-source-field/318c12ea7c36f63e2939bbed536f9dc1848f8aa2cd199a0b7b35c2dbd765c15b/3b4e2ca9a56beb090c0e2af1a919e704c545c77abe02f85dfd9d81f5d13fe44a" "q-162581/ma-source-field/318c12ea7c36f63e2939bbed536f9dc1848f8aa2cd199a0b7b35c2dbd765c15b/48926eb34f1e846b681031fafa97063badde87056b576f4fc700078200d4dc3e" "q-162581/ma-source-field/318c12ea7c36f63e2939bbed536f9dc1848f8aa2cd199a0b7b35c2dbd765c15b/93622583d91246fe5dc138371feb07bd9b70d3000b99f9f7ad6a567421106f61" "q-162581/ma-source-field/318c12ea7c36f63e2939bbed536f9dc1848f8aa2cd199a0b7b35c2dbd765c15b/d8f9fc7f929748e021c371fb9cb157d983a78128d4e44c5c96a0b17cb0ab3cc3" "q-162581/ma-source-field/318c12ea7c36f63e2939bbed536f9dc1848f8aa2cd199a0b7b35c2dbd765c15b/dd703bda1dbe0c1f2061c401d52b12e2735c58169be6d35e33d604614ddea885"] :answer-field/correct "q-162581/ma-source-field/318c12ea7c36f63e2939bbed536f9dc1848f8aa2cd199a0b7b35c2dbd765c15b/dd703bda1dbe0c1f2061c401d52b12e2735c58169be6d35e33d604614ddea885" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-162581" :question/id #uuid "e0d14173-8029-50a6-8f00-d7853c057071" :question/math-academy-id "q-162581" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=0,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=xy\\,i+2y\\,j+{x}^{2}zk,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =y+2+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+y+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+y+2)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,{x}^{2}+y+2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-162581/ma-source-field/318c12ea7c36f63e2939bbed536f9dc1848f8aa2cd199a0b7b35c2dbd765c15b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
